# Bitcoin Intraday Microstructure and Machine Learning Trading Project

## Analysis of high-frequency Bitcoin data and detection of short-term market patterns
This project focuses on the analysis of Bitcoin intraday market behaviour using high-frequency data at one-minute resolution. The objective was to investigate whether very short-term market structures could be identified inside Bitcoin price movements and whether these structures could be transformed into a systematic trading signal through machine learning.
The starting point of the project was a dataset containing almost one million one-minute observations of Bitcoin prices from Binance, including open, high, low, close and volume. The analysis was not designed as a simple price forecasting exercise, but as an attempt to study the microstructure of the Bitcoin market: short-term price pressure, volatility bursts, momentum persistence, volume behaviour, candle structure, intraday patterns and the probability that a movement continues over the following minutes.
The logic behind the project was that, at very short horizons, financial markets are extremely noisy. For this reason, the model was not expected to predict the exact future price of Bitcoin. Instead, the goal was to identify situations in which the probability of a directional movement was slightly higher than random and sufficiently strong to become economically useful after trading costs.



## Feature engineering and microstructure variables
A major part of the project was the construction of explanatory variables capable of summarizing the short-term state of the Bitcoin market. Starting from one-minute OHLCV data, several groups of features were created using only past information, so that the model could not benefit from future data.
The first group of variables captured short-term returns and momentum. Returns over one, five, ten and twenty minutes were calculated to measure whether Bitcoin was showing immediate directional persistence. These variables were useful to understand whether recent price movements contained information about the next short-term move.
The second group focused on volatility and range-based measures. Rolling volatility, normalized high-low ranges, ATR-type indicators, Parkinson volatility and Garman-Klass volatility were used to describe the intensity of price movement and the level of market instability. This was important because Bitcoin can alternate between quiet phases and explosive phases, and the model needed to distinguish between these regimes.
The third group included technical and microstructure-style indicators such as RSI, MACD, Bollinger z-score, distance from rolling highs and lows, distance from VWAP, candle body, upper and lower wick, close location within the candle and volume z-score. These variables were designed to capture whether the price was closing near the high or low of the candle, whether volume was abnormal, whether Bitcoin was extended relative to its recent range, and whether the market was showing signs of short-term pressure.
Additional variables were also introduced to capture time-of-day effects, autocorrelation, skewness, kurtosis and volatility of volatility. These features are relevant because intraday crypto markets can behave differently depending on liquidity, market activity and the presence of sudden volatility clusters.
Overall, the feature engineering phase transformed raw price data into a structured representation of Bitcoin’s short-term market state. This was the foundation for the machine learning model, because the objective was not to feed the algorithm only with prices, but with variables that approximate the internal microstructure of the market.



## Target construction and trading logic
The first modelling approach defined a realistic trading target based on future price movement over a twenty-minute horizon. The model was trained to distinguish between situations where Bitcoin had enough upside potential to justify a long trade and situations where downside movement was stronger. A minimum threshold was introduced to account for fees and slippage, so that the model would not classify insignificant movements as profitable opportunities.
This was an important step because, in high-frequency trading, being slightly correct is not enough. A signal must overcome transaction costs. For this reason, the target was not simply based on whether the next return was positive or negative, but on whether the forward movement was large enough to be economically meaningful.
The dataset was then split chronologically into training, validation and test periods. This was crucial because a random split would have created unrealistic results by mixing past and future observations. The model was trained on the past, calibrated on a validation block and finally tested on the last unseen period. This structure made the experiment closer to a real trading environment.



## Random Forest model and signal extraction
The first model used was a Random Forest classifier. The reason for using this model is that Random Forest can capture nonlinear relationships between market variables without imposing a rigid functional form. This is particularly useful in Bitcoin intraday data, where the relationship between volume, volatility, momentum and future returns is unlikely to be linear.
The first model achieved a test AUC of approximately 0.542, with average precision around 0.543. These values show that the signal was only slightly better than random when measured on all one-minute bars. However, the project correctly moved beyond simple AUC and evaluated the model operationally through probability thresholds.
Instead of trading every prediction, the model searched for high-confidence situations. A threshold was optimized on the validation set and then frozen before being applied to the test set. With a threshold of 0.60, the model traded only around 3.6% of the test bars, skipping more than 96% of observations. This is an important result: the model was not used to trade continuously, but to identify rare situations where the probability signal was stronger.
On the test set, the long signals showed a precision of approximately 63.4%, while short signals showed a precision of approximately 58.7%. This suggests that the model was able to identify some directional information in high-confidence areas. However, when transaction costs were included in a realistic trading backtest, the strategy performed poorly. The weekly backtest showed a negative total return and a strongly negative Sharpe ratio, mainly because frequent position changes generated excessive costs.
This was one of the most important lessons of the project: a model can show statistical predictive ability and still fail economically. In intraday trading, transaction costs, slippage, turnover and execution frequency can destroy a weak edge.



## From one-minute noise to five-minute bars
After observing that the one-minute model was too noisy and unable to survive trading costs, the project introduced a more realistic second stage. The data were resampled from one-minute bars to five-minute bars. This reduced the number of observations but increased the signal-to-noise ratio. The idea was that very short-term one-minute movements are often dominated by random noise, while five-minute bars may provide more stable information about market direction.
In this stage, the model used a thirty-minute prediction horizon, corresponding to six five-minute bars. Additional filters were introduced, including a volatility regime filter to avoid trading in excessively unstable periods, adaptive thresholds and a meta-labeling layer designed to decide whether the primary model’s signal should actually be traded.
The primary Random Forest model improved in terms of AUC, reaching approximately 0.579 on the test set. This confirmed that the five-minute framework contained more usable information than the pure one-minute approach. However, the full strategy with meta-labeling and regime filtering still produced a negative out-of-sample performance. This showed that improving predictive accuracy is not sufficient unless the trading rule, position sizing, holding period and cost structure are also correctly designed.



## Triple Barrier and hold-to-maturity backtest
The most advanced part of the project introduced a Triple Barrier approach inspired by professional machine learning methods for financial trading. Instead of labeling trades only by future direction, the Triple Barrier method defines outcomes based on three events: a take-profit barrier, a stop-loss barrier and a maximum holding period.
This approach is more realistic because every trade is evaluated according to whether it reaches a profit target, hits a loss limit, or expires after a fixed time. In the project, the barriers were linked to ATR, meaning that the profit and loss thresholds adapted to market volatility. This is particularly important for Bitcoin because volatility changes significantly over time.
A Random Forest model was then trained using these Triple Barrier labels. The model achieved a test AUC of approximately 0.564. More importantly, the trading backtest became economically meaningful. Using a high entry threshold of 0.60, a five-minute frequency and a hold-to-maturity structure with a maximum holding period of one hour, the final out-of-sample backtest generated 239 trades. The strategy reached a win rate of approximately 59.8%, an average profit per trade of around 0.0485%, a total return of approximately 11.8%, a positive annualized Sharpe ratio of about 2.25 and a maximum drawdown of around 6.2%.
This was the strongest part of the project because it showed how a weak predictive signal can become more useful when combined with a better labeling method, stricter trade selection and a realistic execution framework. The model was no longer trying to predict every small price movement. Instead, it was used to identify specific situations where the expected trade structure was more favorable.



## Main learning outcomes
The project taught that high-frequency crypto trading is not mainly a problem of finding a model with the highest accuracy. The real challenge is transforming a noisy prediction into an economically robust trading process. A small edge can be useful only if the strategy trades selectively, controls costs, avoids excessive turnover and uses a realistic definition of profit and loss.
The work also showed the importance of separating statistical performance from financial performance. AUC, precision and feature importance are useful diagnostic tools, but they do not automatically imply profitability. A trading model must be evaluated through net returns, transaction costs, drawdown, Sharpe ratio, number of trades, win rate and average profit per trade.
Another important lesson was the role of market microstructure features. Variables related to volume, EMA slopes, candle close location, distance from rolling highs and lows, high-low spread, short-term returns and volatility provided the model with information about the internal state of the market. These features helped the model identify moments where Bitcoin was more likely to experience short-term directional continuation or reversal.
Finally, the project demonstrated that professional quantitative trading requires an iterative research process. The first one-minute model showed limited predictive power and failed after costs. The second stage improved the signal through five-minute aggregation and regime filtering but still did not produce satisfactory net performance. The final Triple Barrier framework produced better results because it aligned the model’s objective with the real economics of trading.



## Professional relevance
This project is relevant for quantitative finance, crypto market research, systematic trading and market microstructure analysis. It demonstrates the ability to work with high-frequency data, engineer predictive features, build machine learning models, avoid temporal leakage, design realistic targets, evaluate transaction costs and translate model outputs into trading rules.
From a professional perspective, the project shows that I can analyze a market not only from a traditional investment perspective, but also from an intraday quantitative perspective. It connects Python programming, financial feature engineering, machine learning, backtesting, risk management and trading strategy design into one coherent workflow.
The main value of the project is not simply the final return, but the research process: starting from raw Bitcoin minute data, building microstructure indicators, testing predictive models, identifying the limits of naïve high-frequency trading, and progressively improving the framework until the model became more aligned with real trading constraints.
